| `\breakseq{fig:architecture;}`{=latex} `\breakseq{canonical\_stimulus}`{=latex} | `\breakseq{output/data/architecture.json;}`{=latex} `\breakseq{formalism:eq:typed\_request,}`{=latex} `\breakseq{formalism:eq:canonical\_generation,}`{=latex} `\breakseq{formalism:eq:encoding\_verification}`{=latex} | Controls: typed request q=(i,θ,s,e) with seed s=0; default generator and delivery-independent canonicalization Objective: no unit-bearing objective quantity is applicable to this architecture diagram; stage identities and provenance fields are recorded in the sidecar | Limitations: The schematic abstracts implementation boundaries and does not represent an observer study or a causal theory of perception. Boundary: The pipeline verifies reproducible media facts, not what a person experiences. `\breakseq{Accessibility:}`{=latex} Stage names, mathematical symbols, and arrow direction are printed directly; color is redundant. |
| `\breakseq{fig:catalog\_matrix;}`{=latex} `\breakseq{source\_supported}`{=latex} | `\breakseq{output/data/catalog\_matrix.json;}`{=latex} `\breakseq{gregory1997visual,}`{=latex} `\breakseq{hirst2020sound,}`{=latex} `\breakseq{bruns2019ventriloquist}`{=latex} | Controls: live taxonomy registry; evidence matrix snapshot; seed not applicable to the catalog diagram Objective: 18 catalog rows; categorical facets and statuses; no observer-level quantity is applicable | Limitations: Taxonomy facets are engineering classifications and remain provisional where the literature supports competing accounts. Boundary: Catalog coverage is bounded to this registered package and does not enumerate all known illusions. `\breakseq{Accessibility:}`{=latex} Every status and facet is written as text; color only reinforces the printed status. |
| `\breakseq{fig:visual\_panel;}`{=latex} `\breakseq{canonical\_stimulus}`{=latex} | `\breakseq{output/data/visual\_panel.json;}`{=latex} `\breakseq{gregory1997visual,}`{=latex} `\breakseq{brugger1999duckrabbit,}`{=latex} `\breakseq{howe2005muller,}`{=latex} `\breakseq{morgan1999poggendorff,}`{=latex} `\breakseq{fisher1967ponzo,}`{=latex} `\breakseq{yildiz2022ponzo,}`{=latex} `\breakseq{kanizsa1976contours,}`{=latex} `\breakseq{mruczek2015ebbinghaus,}`{=latex} `\breakseq{earle1995zollner,}`{=latex} `\breakseq{wertheimer1912motion,}`{=latex} `\breakseq{sekuler1996wertheimer}`{=latex} | Controls: one default typed parameter object per implemented visual entry; seed s=0; dimensions, luminance bounds, grayscale, and quantization controls remain in each generator schema; temporal entries display their first frame but retain sequence metrics Objective: per tile: mean and standard deviation of the declared uncalibrated channel-value proxy, unique raster levels, and canonical SHA-256 digest; temporal entries additionally retain frame count, frame rate, and frame deltas; definitions and units are in the source data JSON | Limitations: The gallery is complete for implemented visual entries in this package, not a complete survey of visual illusions; literature families, historical displays, and DuckRabbit rasters are not pixel-identical by default. Viewing scale, display calibration, viewing distance, and observer conditions can change the relevance of a construction; the gallery does not measure an observer's perceptual report. Boundary: The figure establishes coverage of the package's implemented visual constructions and their deterministic raster facts. It does not establish that any viewer will perceive the named signature, nor that the set is exhaustive of the visual-illusion literature. `\breakseq{Accessibility:}`{=latex} Each tile is labeled by stable illusion ID and accompanied by printed luminance, level-count, and digest fields; color only reinforces grouping and is not required to identify a stimulus. |
| `\breakseq{fig:visual\_sweep;}`{=latex} `\breakseq{physical\_metric}`{=latex} | `\breakseq{output/data/visual\_sweep.json;}`{=latex} `\breakseq{brugger1999duckrabbit,}`{=latex} `\breakseq{gregory1997visual}`{=latex} | Controls: duck_weight ∈ `\breakseq{\{0,.25,.5,.75,1\};}`{=latex} `\breakseq{grayscale=quantization}`{=latex} ∈ `\breakseq{\{2,4,8,16,32\};}`{=latex} seed s=0 Objective: 25 canonical rasters; unique levels, normalized mean luminance, and effective dynamic range per cell | Limitations: The grid is a sensitivity of the construction parameters, not a psychophysical sensitivity curve or a validated observer effect. Boundary: The chosen grid is an engineering sampling of the parameter domain and does not imply an optimal or perceptually uniform spacing. `\breakseq{Accessibility:}`{=latex} Row and column labels state the parameter values; numerical metrics and units are available in the sidecar. |
| `\breakseq{fig:temporal\_sequence;}`{=latex} `\breakseq{physical\_metric}`{=latex} | `\breakseq{output/data/temporal\_sequence.json;}`{=latex} `\breakseq{wertheimer1912motion,}`{=latex} `\breakseq{sekuler1996wertheimer}`{=latex} | Controls: `\breakseq{visual.apparent\_motion}`{=latex} default typed parameters; frame rate and frame count from the canonical sequence; seed s=0 Objective: timestamps in seconds, frame rate in frames/s, frame count, rational timebase, and adjacent-frame normalized pixel difference | Limitations: Frame succession and frame deltas are physical file properties; playback timing, display persistence, and motion reports require a controlled observer task. Boundary: The figure documents temporal structure rather than the presence, direction, or strength of perceived motion. `\breakseq{Accessibility:}`{=latex} Frame index, seconds, frames per second, and normalized-difference labels remain interpretable without color. |
| `\breakseq{fig:audio\_signals;}`{=latex} `\breakseq{physical\_metric}`{=latex} | `\breakseq{output/data/audio\_signals.json;}`{=latex} `\breakseq{shepard1984scale,}`{=latex} `\breakseq{zatorre2005missing,}`{=latex} `\breakseq{deutsch1986tritone,}`{=latex} `\breakseq{repp1997tritone,}`{=latex} `\breakseq{deutsch1974octave,}`{=latex} `\breakseq{warren1970continuity,}`{=latex} `\breakseq{riecke2011continuity}`{=latex} | Controls: default typed audio parameters; per-generator channel layout and sample rate; seed s=0; one-sided FFT display with 48 frequency-labelled peak-magnitude bands Objective: RMS and peak in normalized amplitude; spectral centroid and bandwidth in Hz; sample rate in samples/s; channel layout and canonical SHA-256 digest | Limitations: Playback level, transducer response, dichotic separation, masker design, and listener judgments are external to the canonical buffer. Boundary: The figure compares generated signal properties and literature-linked families; it does not establish pitch, continuity, or stream organization for a listener. `\breakseq{Accessibility:}`{=latex} Waveform, spectral, amplitude, and frequency labels are printed; bar color is not the sole encoding. |
| `\breakseq{fig:audiovisual\_timeline;}`{=latex} `\breakseq{physical\_metric}`{=latex} | `\breakseq{output/data/audiovisual\_timeline.json;}`{=latex} `\breakseq{shams2000sifi,}`{=latex} `\breakseq{hirst2020sound,}`{=latex} `\breakseq{vroomen2004temporal,}`{=latex} `\breakseq{hartcherobrien2011temporal}`{=latex} | Controls: `\breakseq{audiovisual.sound\_induced\_flash}`{=latex} default shared timeline; declared sync offset ΔAV and spatial offset; seed s=0 Objective: video frame-hold `\breakseq{grayscale/weighted-RGB}`{=latex} proxy and mean-channel absolute audio amplitude on shared time in seconds; ΔAV in ms; spatial discrepancy in normalized units; frame and sample clocks in the sidecar | Limitations: Display refresh, audio hardware, event latency, and observer temporal binding are not measured by this figure. Boundary: A shared-clock construction is not evidence that observers bind the events at the declared or any other perceptual time. `\breakseq{Accessibility:}`{=latex} The two channels, time axis, signed offset convention, and normalized spatial discrepancy are labeled in text. |
| `\breakseq{fig:encoding\_verification;}`{=latex} `\breakseq{encoded\_media}`{=latex} | `\breakseq{output/data/encoding\_verification.json;}`{=latex} `\breakseq{engineering:typed\_media\_adapters}`{=latex} | Controls: typed encoding profile selected per artifact; overwrite and backend capability checks; seed inherited from the stimulus request Objective: format availability and verification scope are environment observations; NPZ is exact while other formats are decoded and tolerance-checked | Limitations: Backend availability, codec versions, container metadata, and lossy tolerances are environment-dependent; playback software can expose additional behavior. Boundary: A successful encode or decode does not validate an observer effect or guarantee cross-device perceptual equivalence. `\breakseq{Accessibility:}`{=latex} Format, backend, preserved fact, and verification columns are textual and remain usable without color. |
| `\breakseq{fig:synthetic\_psychophysics;}`{=latex} `\breakseq{synthetic\_model\_output}`{=latex} | `\breakseq{output/data/synthetic\_psychophysics.json;}`{=latex} `\breakseq{observer:preregistered\_estimands}`{=latex} | Controls: duck_weight comparison against fixed reference; serialized feature weights and temperature; seed s=0 Objective: model probability, null baseline, ablations, temperature sensitivity and exact-self control; full feature values, specifications and canonical stimulus digests; `\breakseq{human\_data=false;}`{=latex} `\breakseq{training\_data=none}`{=latex} | Limitations: The model is hand-specified, has no training data, has not been calibrated against observers, and does not estimate a human threshold or effect size. Its probabilities and the finite configuration range are model outputs, not participant responses, confidence intervals, or a psychometric function. Boundary: This diagnostic tests deterministic orchestration and metamorphic input-output behavior only; empirical psychophysics remains future work. `\breakseq{Accessibility:}`{=latex} Model scale, reference line, model identity, and `\texttt{\breakseq{human\textbackslash{}\_data=false}}`{=latex} are printed; color is not needed to interpret the curve. |
| `\breakseq{fig:claim\_boundary;}`{=latex} `\breakseq{source\_supported}`{=latex} | `\breakseq{output/data/claim\_boundary.json;}`{=latex} `\breakseq{formalism:eq:observer\_estimand,}`{=latex} `\breakseq{formalism:eq:canonical\_digest}`{=latex} | Controls: claim levels in the taxonomy and manifest; seed not applicable to the explanatory diagram Objective: no unit-bearing objective quantity is applicable; stage definitions and evidence boundaries are preserved in the sidecar | Limitations: The diagram is a documentation contract and does not replace a preregistered observer study or empirical data. Boundary: A deterministic artifact can support a future hypothesis but cannot supply the observer data required to test it. `\breakseq{Accessibility:}`{=latex} Each stage is named, numbered, and described in text; the boundary rule is readable without color. |
| `\breakseq{fig:scholarship\_map;}`{=latex} `\breakseq{source\_supported}`{=latex} | `\breakseq{output/data/scholarship\_map.json;}`{=latex} `\breakseq{gregory1997visual,}`{=latex} `\breakseq{brugger1999duckrabbit,}`{=latex} `\breakseq{yildiz2022ponzo,}`{=latex} `\breakseq{hirst2020sound,}`{=latex} `\breakseq{bruns2019ventriloquist}`{=latex} | Controls: checked-in evidence matrix; source roles and entry statuses; audit date recorded in `\breakseq{data/evidence\_matrix.json}`{=latex} Objective: one evidence row per catalog entry (18 entries); source-tier counts, exact claim text, engineering basis, limitation, and gap status | Limitations: The offline snapshot validates citation and lineage structure; live resolver status is a separate audit, and classifications can remain contested. Boundary: Scholarship coverage bounds the package's evidence record and does not establish that any generated stimulus produces a universal percept. `\breakseq{Accessibility:}`{=latex} Source roles, claims, limitations, and statuses are printed or available in the sidecar; no category is encoded by color alone. |
| `\breakseq{fig:formalism\_traceability;}`{=latex} `\breakseq{physical\_metric}`{=latex} | `\breakseq{output/data/formalism\_traceability.json;}`{=latex} `\breakseq{formalism:eq:canonical\_digest,}`{=latex} `\breakseq{formalism:eq:clock\_definition,}`{=latex} `\breakseq{formalism:eq:objective\_statistics}`{=latex} | Controls: formalism registry version; seed not applicable to the traceability diagram Objective: nine equation records with implementation, test, figure, and claim-level fields; no unit-bearing measurement is plotted | Limitations: Traceability records documentation and verification scope; it does not establish the truth of an observer-level theory. Boundary: A linked equation and test can show an implemented contract, not a validated perceptual law. `\breakseq{Accessibility:}`{=latex} Equation labels, code paths, tests, and figure labels are written as text. |
| `\breakseq{fig:metrics\_dashboard;}`{=latex} `\breakseq{physical\_metric}`{=latex} | `\breakseq{output/data/metrics\_dashboard.json;}`{=latex} `\breakseq{formalism:eq:objective\_statistics,}`{=latex} `\breakseq{formalism:eq:temporal\_spectral\_metrics}`{=latex} | Controls: default canonical artifacts; metric computation version and tolerance recorded in the source-data sidecar; seed s=0 Objective: finite objective values with units: normalized `\breakseq{luminance/amplitude,}`{=latex} Hz, normalized pixel difference, ms, counts, and durations | Limitations: Metrics are media properties and do not encode pitch, size, motion, localization, binding, or other observer `\breakseq{interpretations.}`{=latex} Boundary: Metric reproducibility is a package property; interpretation as perception requires a separate observer design and data. `\breakseq{Accessibility:}`{=latex} Every numerical value is paired with a printed unit or an explicit dimensionless definition. |
| `\breakseq{fig:parameter\_domains;}`{=latex} `\breakseq{canonical\_stimulus}`{=latex} | `\breakseq{output/data/parameter\_domains.json;}`{=latex} `\breakseq{formalism:eq:typed\_request}`{=latex} | Controls: parameter schema version and validated scalar domains; seed not applicable to the domain diagram Objective: dimensionless, pixel, level, Hz, samples/s, frames/s, ms, and normalized spatial units as listed per parameter | Limitations: The intervals are software validation bounds and do not encode safe listening levels, display limits, or psychophysical thresholds. Boundary: A valid parameter is a reproducible construction request, not evidence that the requested value is perceptually effective. `\breakseq{Accessibility:}`{=latex} Type names, endpoints, units, and roles are printed; interval color is redundant. |
| `\breakseq{fig:observer\_protocol;}`{=latex} `\breakseq{observer\_hypothesis}`{=latex} | `\breakseq{output/data/observer\_protocol.json;}`{=latex} `\breakseq{observer:preregistered\_estimands}`{=latex} | Controls: study design and deterministic randomization seed recorded in the sidecar; synthetic response generation is separate from human data Objective: trial counts, condition structure, estimand templates, response schemas, and model families; participant outcomes are not applicable | Limitations: The scaffold specifies future data collection and analysis but supplies no participant responses, fitted coefficients, power claim, or validated observer effect. Boundary: The protocol requires ethical review, task and calibration specifications, and preregistration before human use, not evidence that the proposed effect exists. `\breakseq{Accessibility:}`{=latex} Every protocol step and the no-participant-data boundary is stated in text; arrows and labels remain legible without color. |
